WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.480
Hermes Agent is one of the most powerful

00:10:00.057 --> 00:10:01.487
are in a file called skill.md.

00:10:01.707 --> 00:10:09.947
They have a Yamo front matter, which is basically just like a little front matter that that tells the agent, hey, this skill does this, so use it for X Y and Z.

00:10:10.057 --> 00:10:19.347
And that's basically a concept called progressive disclosure, which helps make sure that you're not loading full skills, full context bloat into a session if you don't actually need to use that skill.

00:10:19.467 --> 00:10:22.847
So Hermes will understand the use case for the skill.

00:10:22.957 --> 00:10:28.570
It will then invoke the skill and read it, and then it will ship that information into the session and then invoke the skill and do what you need.

00:10:28.917 --> 00:10:37.767
And what's cool about the Hermes agent is that if you're doing things frequently, if you forget, hey, let's build a skill out of this, it will analyze conversations, it will analyze your workflow, and it will turn things into skills.

00:10:37.897 --> 00:10:41.527
And then of course, as you use skills more and more, if you're giving feedback, it's going to update those skills as well.

00:10:41.697 --> 00:10:46.127
As you can see, these exdraw diagrams keep messing up right here, which is basically just the beginner nuance.

00:10:46.407 --> 00:10:51.137
So memory equals what to remember, skill equals how to do it again.

00:10:51.527 --> 00:10:54.387
Hermes can create or patch skills after real work.

00:10:54.767 --> 00:10:55.727
So super cool.

00:10:56.407 --> 00:10:57.657
And of course, there's a skills hub.

00:10:57.927 --> 00:11:02.570
So any skills you've already built can be used in Hermes, or you can go to the skills hub.

00:11:02.570 --> 00:11:06.867
And you can see that there's over 520 community skills.

00:11:06.867 --> 00:11:08.307
There's different categories.

00:11:08.517 --> 00:11:13.807
So you can definitely search through here to see what you can add to your Hermes to make it even more powerful.

00:11:14.057 --> 00:11:20.997
It looks like there's actually 16 right here that are anthropic um official skills.

00:11:21.347 --> 00:11:23.157
We have canvas design, front-end design.

00:11:23.507 --> 00:11:25.347
We have a skill creator skill.

00:11:25.807 --> 00:11:28.627
So you can pull all of these into your Hermes agent.

00:11:28.947 --> 00:11:30.627
Super, super easily.

00:11:30.627 --> 00:11:34.337
You just have to basically run this command or, you know, you could drop in this URL

00:11:34.907 --> 00:11:37.437
to your Hermes agent and say, hey, install this skill right now.

00:11:37.667 --> 00:11:38.777
All right, so that's pillar two.

00:11:39.197 --> 00:11:40.997
Pillar three is the soul.

00:11:41.407 --> 00:11:45.777
Now, Soul MD basically shapes the assistant.

00:11:45.977 --> 00:11:48.977
This shapes your Hermes agent.

00:11:48.977 --> 00:11:53.687
So that if you have six different Hermes agents, they all have basically a different vibe.

00:11:54.007 --> 00:11:55.397
Some can be concise, some can be rude.

00:11:55.757 --> 00:11:58.847
Some can be I don't know.

00:11:58.847 --> 00:12:06.967
But anyways, the soul that MD is another markdown file that gets put into the context of the agent.

00:12:07.147 --> 00:12:09.577
And now it's able to just have a bit of a personality.

00:12:09.577 --> 00:12:16.197
So if you're also letting other people interact with your Hermes, they will feel the personality if your Hermes is, you know, commenting on YouTube videos like mine.

00:12:16.757 --> 00:12:18.387
There will be some sort of personality.

00:12:18.897 --> 00:12:21.677
The one in my YouTube comments, I told it to be like very sarcastic, but not rude.

00:12:21.877 --> 00:12:26.407
And by the way, guys, it's not super, super important that you need to like know what a skill file looks like or know exactly what a markdown file looks like.

00:12:26.607 --> 00:12:28.167
But don't get intimidated.

00:12:28.427 --> 00:12:29.877
Here is a skill.

00:12:30.317 --> 00:12:31.787
So this is skill.md.

00:12:32.017 --> 00:12:33.477
This one's called generate image.

00:12:33.867 --> 00:12:36.467
So everything in between these two lines up here, that is the the Yamo front matter that I was talking about.

00:12:36.777 --> 00:12:37.997
This is what your agent reads.

00:12:38.217 --> 00:12:41.467
In order to understand, okay, should I use this skill or not?

00:12:41.767 --> 00:12:45.737
If it then decides, yes, I should use this skill, then it's going to read all of this other stuff,

00:12:45.917 --> 00:12:48.127
which is just markdown, and markdown just basically means that it's using like

00:12:48.517 --> 00:12:53.847
headers and bullets, and it's just a way for agents to read structure within a block of text.

00:12:53.847 --> 00:12:55.057
Like this means bold.

00:12:55.487 --> 00:12:58.687
Anyways, that's what a markdown file is, and that's what a skill file looks like.

00:12:58.917 --> 00:13:02.257
So a soul file would look like that, but there wouldn't be that Yamo front matter.

00:13:02.527 --> 00:13:08.197
But as you might have guessed, the soul file will also evolve over time based on the feedback that you're giving your Hermes.

00:13:08.767 --> 00:13:10.247
So what is pillar number four?

00:13:10.547 --> 00:13:16.407
This is where it gets super cool, and this is one of the main value props for me over something like cloud code,

00:13:16.887 --> 00:13:22.947
is the fact that I can just say, hey, spin up a cron job to do this at this time, and it just does it.

00:13:23.027 --> 00:13:26.587
Cloud code obviously you have your routines, you have your loops, but that usually requires you to leave some sort of infrastructure on.

00:13:26.967 --> 00:13:30.867
Unless you're using like that that new cloud routine, but you're only limited to 15 of those, at least on the the Max plan,

00:13:31.237 --> 00:13:32.567
15 of those a day.

00:13:32.837 --> 00:13:37.417
So once again, Hermes doesn't replace cloud code for me, but it's kind of my on-the-go

00:13:37.417 --> 00:13:40.157
spin-up things really quick, and that's why I love it.

00:13:40.157 --> 00:13:44.227
So cron's turn on Hermes from reactive into a proactive scheduled automation.

00:13:44.587 --> 00:13:46.547
And you're still getting that full agentic loop if you want it.

00:13:46.797 --> 00:13:52.007
So you could say in natural language, hey, every morning at 6:00 a.m., I want you to do X, Y, and Z.

00:13:52.327 --> 00:13:56.427
It will go ahead and use a skill and use its tools to create that cron job.

00:13:56.657 --> 00:14:02.497
And then when that time hits, it will basically invoke like a fresh isolated session.

00:14:02.747 --> 00:14:05.507
It doesn't inherit any of the context that you're currently, you know, having that conversation about.

00:14:05.897 --> 00:14:07.967
And then it will just run that skill.

00:14:08.177 --> 00:14:12.007
After that happens, it will send that result back to the original chat, and it will

00:14:12.487 --> 00:14:17.657
maybe update any local files or do whatever it needs to do based on the skill requirements.

00:14:17.947 --> 00:14:24.447
So some useful pieces of advice down here, context_from is to pass one job output into another.

00:14:24.847 --> 00:14:31.327
Workdir is, you know, work directory, and it runs tools from a project folder.

00:14:31.697 --> 00:14:36.547
And then you can do this flag for no agent, which is just a script, so hey, I just want you to run this python script.

00:14:36.907 --> 00:14:40.447
I don't want the agentic harness loop inside of that.

00:14:40.447 --> 00:14:41.997
I just want the script to be ran.

00:14:42.167 --> 00:14:45.807
So if you think back to the WAT framework, workflow, agent, tools,

00:14:45.957 --> 00:14:49.477
you'd basically just be deploying the workflow on something like modal, you're not deploying the agent as well.

00:14:49.757 --> 00:14:53.457
So safety nuance, cron sessions cannot recursively create more cron jobs.

00:14:53.857 --> 00:14:55.667
So the prompts need to be self-contained.

00:14:55.967 --> 00:15:02.507
And if any of this is starting to feel a little bit overwhelming, I just want you to understand this terminology so that when we hop into the setup and the onboarding,

00:15:02.737 --> 00:15:03.967
it all clicks a little bit better.

00:15:04.537 --> 00:15:05.577
So just stick with me.

00:15:05.747 --> 00:15:12.817
All right, and then the last pillar here is the self-improving loop Hermes improves when useful experience gets persisted as memory, skills, and searchable history.

00:15:13.257 --> 00:15:17.067
So if you think about the loop like this, you do the work, the agent learns,

00:15:17.477 --> 00:15:21.787
you save things to memory or to agents.md or to, you know, user.md.

00:15:22.097 --> 00:15:26.657
And then you turn those repeatable steps into skills or your preferences into more memory.

00:15:26.977 --> 00:15:31.327
And then, the agent's able to search past sessions when old context matters.

00:15:31.577 --> 00:15:34.347
And then you basically just go in that loop over and over.

00:15:34.507 --> 00:15:38.407
So the more you use your Hermes agent, the better it's going to get and the more it's going to understand you.

00:15:38.657 --> 00:15:44.477
So the nuance here is that automatic does not mean magic. The loop works best when the user corrects Hermes,

00:15:44.897 --> 00:15:50.237
asks it to save things to memory, and lets it create and update skills after you've done some complex work.

00:15:50.457 --> 00:15:56.547
